A||N||5||ChadW13||Intermittent stumbling 2000-3500rpm||||9||
Z||000000||ChadW13||12-05-1999||05:11 PM||||A couple of weeks ago my 86' SE V6 would stumble in the lower Rpm's.  I unplugged the battery for 1 night and the car ran ok for about a week.  Well it has started again.  I'm not sure if it is temperature related, vacuum, fuel injectors, electrical, or some sensor. When pushed passed 3500Rpm, the engine seemed to pull fine. I don't understand when the computer resets, the problem goes away temporarily. ||||reg||9
Z||000001||theogre||12-05-1999||07:50 PM||||start at the usual search for vacuum leaks yet?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||12-05-1999||08:10 PM||||Does it have the same problem when the engine is cold? If not, I would say your O2 sensor is shot.<P>When you reset the ECM, it goes into <I>learning mode</I> for a period of time. It collects information from the sensors and will calculate appropriate air/fuel mixture. During this learning mode it will use a default setting. If one of the sensors is shot, it gets wrong readings and you'll notice that once the ECM switches back to normal operation (because only then will it use the calculated mixture). So my guess is it is one of the sensors. Suspicious are the MAP sensor and the O2 sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadW13||12-05-1999||10:52 PM||||Yes, it will usually die at startup and lightly stumble when it is cold but seems to get worse as it(the engine) gets warmer.  Also, Within the last week the outside temperature has risen.  If it is the O2 sensor, will this show up in the engine codes? (maybe?) My check engine light has not come on and I have not checked the codes yet.  Also, this problem started after I got the car out of the transmission shop where they had to pull the tranny.  Could they have messed up the O2 sensor?  I really appreciate your input.  Thanks!!  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||js||12-10-1999||01:18 AM||||Your EGR valve and/or solenoid may be faulty. The EGR valve can develop a weak diaphram, and it allows opening of the valve with less vacuum than intended. The EGR valve opens at a lower RPM (%throttle) than it is supposed to, and causes the stumbling.This then affects the o2 sensor signals to the ECM. Above 3500 RPM, (greater % throttle)the EGR valve is open when it is should be and no stumbling will be present. The amount of vacuum supplied to the EGR is related to the throttle position. <P>If you have one of the aftermarket EGR valves, the diaphram can be adjusted to open at a different vacuum amount. These EGR valves have the circular paper sticker on the center top of the valve.If you have the original GM EGR valve, it probably needs replacement.<P>However, before doing any of this, the solenoid may be the problem. Sometimes the solenoid becomes slightly corroded inside and will not operate properly. <P>EGR valves can be around $30 to $50, and the solenoids I hear are around $100. Instead, your present valve and or solenoid may be repairable.<P>If anyone is interested, I can e-mail repair procedures.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||12-10-1999||06:26 AM||||I am interested in getting a copy of the procedure.<P>Thanks,<BR>Carroll||||reg||5
